{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791395943,"nonce":"e2mrWPYd0xHJy9xdKjVaUS9OZxg=","title":"HIR: vectorize integer reductions computed from a loop counter","message":"Goal: close the Cranelift execution gap to LLVM while retaining interpreter-first startup and avoiding LLVM promotion costs in short runs. LLVM remains the speed reference, not the optimization target.\n\nActual HIR for Lua inlined_call/free_function_call is two phis (i, sum), inclusive i\u003c=99999999, sum += i \u0026 7, i += 1. auto_vectorize rejects the inclusive bound; even with a strict bound it deliberately rejects expressions from the induction variable because it has no vector of successive counter values, and rejects reductions with no array accesses. LLVM-off NUC baseline 9bc3fcd0: inlined_call kernel52.611/wall72.997ms vs LLVM14.468/35.699; free_function_call53.238/73.653 vs LLVM14.563/36.810.\n\nExtend shared vectorization with safe exclusive-bound normalization for constant inclusive bounds and vector induction for integer arithmetic reductions. Preserve scalar tails, overflow semantics, live-out values, OSR transfers, unknown/unsafe loop shapes, and full checksums. Validate native SIMD on x86_64 and ARM64, compare same-executable enable/disable, saved baseline, and LLVM reference at short and long trip counts. Include transformation cost in wall time; no threshold changes.","files":null}]}